  </div>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <strong>2. Do Not Rely on Price as a Quality Signal</strong>
    The Pearson correlation of −0.133 definitively shows that higher prices do not produce higher ratings. Management should decouple pricing strategy from perceived-quality assumptions. Instead, invest in editorial quality, cover design, and marketing as the true drivers of rating performance.
  </div>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <strong>3. Double Down on Fiction Engagement Playbooks</strong>
    Fiction generates 73% more reviews and higher ratings at lower prices. The data suggests Fiction audiences are more emotionally invested and socially vocal. Publishers should prioritize community-building, fan events, and social campaigns for Fiction titles to amplify this organic engagement advantage.
  </div>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <strong>4. Protect Non-Fiction Premium Pricing with Clear Value Justification</strong>
    Non-Fiction commands a 37% price premium ($14.84 vs. $10.85) but scores lower on satisfaction. This gap is acceptable for reference and professional titles (e.g., APA Manual, SAT guides) where demand is inelastic. For general-interest Non-Fiction, however, the $16–$20 band shows the lowest ratings (4.50). Keep general Non-Fiction under $15 unless the content has demonstrable professional or educational value.
  </div>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <strong>5. Exploit the 2015 Pricing Reset as a Permanent Benchmark</strong>
    The 28.8% price drop in 2015 coincided with rising ratings and sustained review growth. This was not a temporary promotional blip — it marked a structural shift in consumer price expectations. Treat $10–$12 as the new normal for mass-market bestsellers, not a discount tier.
  </div>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <strong>6. Invest in Series and Evergreen Backlist</strong>
